.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\">\n                            \n                            <p>There's a new form of boron — and it's really weird</p>\n                            <p><span>News</span> <span><time datetime=\"2026-09-23\">23 SEP 26</time></span></p>\n                        </a>\n                    </li>\n                \n            </ul>\n        </section>\n    \n</section>\n\n\n\n                    \n                        \n                    \n                </div>\n            </article>\n\n            \n        </div></DIV>","content_text":"RESEARCH BRIEFINGS 23 September 2026 Non-uniformities in the layer of passivator molecules, which are used to fix defects and protect the surface of perovskite material in solar cells, can limit the durability of the resulting modules. Large modules treated instead with chemically stable lead carboxylate passivators were efficient, reliable and had a higher yield than did commercial silicon modules. This is a summary of: Xu, D. et al. Lead carboxylates passivation for metre-scale perovskite solar modules. Nature 657, 918–924 (2026). doi: https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-026-02937-z‘Expert opinion’ is published under a CC BY 4.0 licence. ReferencesGreen, M. A. et al. Joule 10, 102494 (2026).Article Google Scholar Xiong, Z. et al. Science 390, 638–642 (2025).Article PubMed Google Scholar Jiang, W. et al. Nature 646, 95–101 (2025).Article PubMed Google Scholar Li, Z. et al. Nature Commun. 17, 7418 (2026).Article PubMed Google Scholar Download references Related Articles Read the paper: Lead carboxylates passivation for metre-scale perovskite solar modules Vaporization trick enables ‘tandem’ solar cells to be made at lower temperatures Uniform film formation helps tandem solar cells work better for longer Subjects Latest on: Renewable energy Materials science Lead carboxylates passivation for metre-scale perovskite solar modules Article 12 AUG 26 Photocatalytic water splitting by 2D polymer with out-of-plane carrier flow Article 29 JUL 26 Electronic-resonance enhanced molecule for perovskite solar cells Article 22 JUL 26 Sequence-encoded hexagonal lattices in multichannel peptide nanofibrils Article 23 SEP 26 Stacking-induced direct band gap in CVD-grown 1H MoS2 bilayers Article 23 SEP 26 There's a new form of boron — and it's really weird News 23 SEP 26","fetched_at":"2026-09-24T16:26:16.565Z","ok":true,"blocks":[{"html_en":"<li data-test=\"article-category\"><span>RESEARCH BRIEFINGS</span></li>","sentences":[{"en":"RESEARCH BRIEFINGS","zh":null}]},{"html_en":"<li>\n                        <time datetime=\"2026-09-23\">23 September 2026</time>\n                    </li>","sentences":[{"en":"23 September 2026","zh":null}]},{"html_en":"<p>\n                    Non-uniformities in the layer of passivator molecules, which are used to fix defects and protect the surface of perovskite material in solar cells, can limit the durability of the resulting modules. Large modules treated instead with chemically stable lead carboxylate passivators were efficient, reliable and had a higher yield than did commercial silicon modules.\n                </p>","sentences":[{"en":"Non-uniformities in the layer of passivator molecules, which are used to fix defects and protect the surface of perovskite material in solar cells, can limit the durability of the resulting modules.","zh":null},{"en":"Large modules treated instead with chemically stable lead carboxylate passivators were efficient, reliable and had a higher yield than did commercial silicon modules.","zh":null}]},{"html_en":"<p>This is a summary of: <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10994-7\" data-track=\"click\" data-label=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10994-7\" data-track-category=\"body text link\">Xu, D. <i>et al</i>. Lead carboxylates passivation for metre-scale perovskite solar modules. <i>Nature</i> <b>657</b>, 918–924 (2026)</a>.</p>","sentences":[{"en":"This is a summary of: Xu, D.","zh